# Git: commit and push

When you finish a task that modifies project files (code, config, assets meant to be tracked):

1. Run `git status` and stage **only intentional changes** (exclude local artifacts like `*.tsbuildinfo`, `.env`, editor junk).
2. Commit with a **clear, complete-sentence** message describing what changed and why.
3. Push to **`origin main`** (or the branch the user is on) so deploys (e.g. Railway) can run.

Do this **automatically** at the end of the task—do not wait for the user to say “push” unless they explicitly want to skip version control.

If there is nothing to commit, say so briefly instead of making an empty commit unless the user asked for a no-op deploy trigger.
